<p class="gna-lede">Most broken noses heal by themselves within about three weeks and need no operation. Treatment is considered when the nose is still visibly crooked once the swelling has gone down, when breathing stays blocked, or, urgently, when blood collects inside the septum (a septal haematoma). If the bones need resetting, this is usually done within about 7 to 14 days; problems that remain after that are corrected with planned surgery months later.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Most broken noses need only first aid and time.</li>
    <li>A soft, purplish swelling inside the nostril can be a septal haematoma and needs same-day treatment.</li>
    <li>Swelling hides the true shape for a few days, so the decision about resetting is usually made at around 3 to 7 days.</li>
    <li>Resetting the bones (closed reduction) is usually done within roughly 7 to 14 days in adults, and sooner in children, before the bones set.</li>
    <li>A nose that heals crooked or blocked can be corrected later with septorhinoplasty, usually after six months or more.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="signs">Is my nose broken? Common signs</h2>
<p>The nose is the most commonly broken bone in the face. In India, falls, road-traffic accidents, sports such as cricket and kabaddi, and assaults are the usual causes. Signs of a broken nose include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Pain, swelling and tenderness over the bridge of the nose</li>
  <li>Bruising around one or both eyes</li>
  <li>A nosebleed at the time of injury</li>
  <li>A crunching or crackling feeling when the nose is touched</li>
  <li>A blocked feeling on one or both sides</li>
  <li>A change in shape: a bend, a flattened bridge or a new bump</li>
  <li>A cut over the bridge of the nose</li>
</ul>
<p>An X-ray is rarely needed for a broken nose on its own; the diagnosis is made by examination. A CT scan is arranged if there are signs of other facial fractures, such as double vision, numbness of the cheek or a change in your bite. If your eye is affected, read our guide to <a href="/blogs/orbital-fracture-eye-socket-symptoms-treatment/">orbital (eye socket) fractures</a>.</p>

<h2 id="first-aid">First aid in the first 48 hours</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Nosebleed:</strong> sit up, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose just above the nostrils for 10 to 15 minutes without letting go. Do not tip your head back.</li>
  <li><strong>Swelling:</strong> hold an ice pack wrapped in a cloth on the nose for up to 15 minutes at a time, several times a day.</li>
  <li><strong>Sleep with your head raised</strong> on extra pillows.</li>
  <li><strong>Pain relief:</strong> paracetamol is usually suitable; ask your doctor what is safe for you.</li>
  <li><strong>Do not</strong> try to straighten the nose yourself, blow your nose, or rest heavy glasses on the bridge while it is swollen.</li>
  <li>Avoid strenuous exercise for about two weeks and contact sports for at least six weeks.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/blogs/facial-trauma-first-aid-steps-everyone-must-know-in-emergencies/">facial trauma first aid guide</a> covers other facial injuries, including cuts and broken teeth.</p>

<h2 id="emergencies">Septal haematoma and other emergencies</h2>
<p>The septum, the wall between the nostrils, is made of cartilage covered by a thin lining that supplies its blood. After an injury, blood can collect between the cartilage and the lining. This is a <strong>septal haematoma</strong>. Without a blood supply the cartilage can become infected and break down within days, leaving a hole in the septum or a collapsed, "saddle-shaped" bridge that is much harder to repair later.</p>
<p>A haematoma shows as a soft, purplish or dark red swelling inside one or both nostrils, often with both sides completely blocked and worsening pain, sometimes with fever. It is treated the same day by draining the blood through a small cut inside the nose, usually under local anaesthesia, followed by packing or soft splints and antibiotics.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Go to the nearest emergency department or call 108 now if, after a nose injury, there is:</strong> a swelling inside the nostril with both sides blocked; a nosebleed that will not stop after 15 to 20 minutes of firm pressure; clear, watery fluid dripping from the nose, which can signal a skull injury; a severe headache, vomiting, drowsiness or confusion; double vision or eye pain; or a large or deep wound. Do not drive yourself.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="why-wait">Why doctors often wait a few days</h2>
<p>In the first days, swelling hides the true shape of the nose, and it is easy either to miss a bend or to worry about one that is really just swelling. For this reason, unless there is an emergency, the nose is usually re-examined at around three to seven days, once the swelling has started to settle. Seek advice earlier if the swelling is not starting to go down after three days, breathing remains difficult or nosebleeds keep recurring.</p>
<p>At this visit the surgeon looks at the nose from the front, side and above, feels the nasal bones, and examines the inside of the nose with a light to check the septum and look for a haematoma. Breathing through each nostril is tested, and the eyes, cheeks and bite are checked so that other facial injuries are not missed.</p>
<p>Bring a recent photograph of your face taken before the injury, such as a selfie from your phone. It helps the surgeon judge how much the shape has really changed.</p>

<h2 id="resetting">Resetting the nose: the 7–14 day window</h2>
<p>If the bones have shifted and the nose looks crooked, they can often be moved back into place without any cuts. This is called <strong>closed reduction</strong> or manipulation. Using fine instruments and their fingers, the surgeon guides the bones and septum back into line, then applies an external splint for about a week. It can be done under local anaesthesia or under a short general anaesthetic, and you usually go home the same day.</p>
<p>Timing matters because the bones begin to set within a couple of weeks. In adults, closed reduction is usually performed once swelling has settled and within about 7 to 14 days of the injury; some guidance allows up to about three weeks. In children, whose bones heal faster, it is usually done sooner. Occasionally, when the patient is seen within the first few hours before swelling develops, the nose can be reset straight away.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Time since injury</th><th>What usually happens</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>First hours</td><td>First aid; emergency assessment if any warning sign is present</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Days 1–3</td><td>Swelling peaks; the inside of the nose should be checked for a haematoma</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Days 3–7</td><td>Review once swelling settles; decision on whether resetting is needed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Days 7–14 (adults)</td><td>Closed reduction, if needed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>After about 3 weeks</td><td>Bones have begun to set; correction is deferred</td></tr>
    <tr><td>About 6 months or later</td><td>Septorhinoplasty if the nose remains crooked or blocked</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Closed reduction often improves the shape, but it does not always restore the nose exactly as it was, and it may not correct a septum that has been knocked badly out of line. Possible problems include persistent crookedness, continued blockage, nosebleeds and the need for further surgery later.</p>
<h3>After closed reduction</h3>
<p>Expect some extra swelling and bruising for a few days and a blocked feeling while the splint is on. Keep the splint dry, sleep with your head raised and avoid blowing your nose until you are told it is safe. Most people return to desk work or college within a few days. Avoid contact sports for at least six weeks, because the bones can be knocked out of place again while they heal, and consider face protection, such as a cricket helmet with a grille, when you return.</p>

