<h2 id="later-surgery">When later surgery is needed</h2>
<p>Some people need a further operation, called <strong>septorhinoplasty</strong>, because:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The injury was not seen in time for closed reduction</li>
  <li>Closed reduction improved but did not fully correct the shape</li>
  <li>The septum is bent and the nose stays blocked</li>
  <li>The bridge has collapsed after a septal haematoma or a severe fracture</li>
</ul>
<p>This operation is usually planned at least six months after the injury, once the bones and soft tissues have fully healed. The nasal bones are carefully re-cut and repositioned, the septum is straightened, and cartilage grafts are used where support needs rebuilding. You can read how this differs from other nasal operations in <a href="/blogs/septoplasty-vs-rhinoplasty-difference/">septoplasty vs rhinoplasty</a>, and what to expect afterwards in our <a href="/blogs/rhinoplasty-recovery-timeline-swelling-guide/">rhinoplasty recovery timeline</a>.</p>
<p>Septorhinoplasty carries the risks of any nasal surgery, including bleeding, infection, prolonged swelling, asymmetry, persistent blockage and dissatisfaction with the result, and a few patients choose a revision. Results vary from person to person. Surgery to reconstruct a nose damaged in an accident may be covered by health insurance, subject to your policy terms and pre-authorisation, so keep your emergency records and any medico-legal paperwork from the time of injury.</p>

<h2 id="children">Broken noses in children</h2>
<p>Children's noses contain more cartilage and less bone, so a fracture can be harder to spot, and children are more prone to septal haematoma. After any significant knock, the inside of a child's nose should be checked by a doctor, even if the outside looks normal. Because an injury can affect how the nose grows, children are followed up over time. Bones are reset if needed, but a definitive cosmetic operation is usually postponed until the nose has finished growing, unless breathing is significantly affected.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>Seek same-day care for any of the warning signs above. Otherwise, arrange a specialist review within a few days of the injury if your nose looks crooked, feels blocked once the initial swelling has eased, or keeps bleeding. Being seen within the first week keeps the option of closed reduction open.</p>
